 encountering you in a fresh, new, deeper way.

91
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,600
 Pray all this in the name of Jesus, amen.

92
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:48,460
 In 2009, I got a phone call from Watermark

93
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:50,360
 and they asked if I would come up and interview.

94
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:53,120
 And they had this role that didn't exist

95
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,520
 or it doesn't exist now, kind of morphed into the Institute.

96
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:56,760
 But basically they pitched,

97
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:57,980
 hey, if you come here for a year,

98
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:00,000
 we will pay you and disciple you,

99
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,280
 which sounds like a pretty sweet gig.

100
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,000
 And so I prayed about it, thought, man,

101
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,900
 I didn't even know if I wanted to be in ministry.

102
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:07,440
 But as I prayed through it, thought,

103
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:08,960
 man, I think I'm supposed to do that.

104
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:12,800
 And so I had a chance to join here and get paid for a year.

105
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:14,480
 And they also said, we're gonna give you free housing,

106
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:15,900
 which is good because they didn't tell me

107
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:18,400
 how much they were gonna pay and it wasn't much.

108
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,860
 And, but they did hook me up with free housing.

109
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,100
 So they said, this is the family

110
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:22,940
 that you're gonna live with.

111
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:24,600
 And here's the back house that you're gonna stay in.

112
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:25,760
 And so I didn't know this family.

113
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:28,520
 I show up at their house, get to meet them and I pull up

114
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:31,120
 and you guys, this reference is gonna be lost on you,

115
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:33,520
 but there used to be a show called Fresh Prince of Bel Air.

116
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,500
 Anybody know that song?

117
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,340
 There's a song that is a part of that,

118
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:39,340
 where he talks about pulling up to the house

119
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:42,200
 about seven or eight and him just being mind blown

120
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:44,160
 at the house that he was pulling up to.

121
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:46,760
 I lived that experience.

122
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,120
 The street that I pulled up to is a street in Dallas

123
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,260
 known as Billionaire's Row.

124
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:52,640
 It's straight lane.

125
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:54,560
 And I'm looking at these houses like,

126
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,460
 the people who own this,

127
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:59,320
 will you just adopt me as your new child

128
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:00,760
 and me be a part of your family?

129
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:01,600
 I don't ever wanna leave here.

130
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,140
 It's all downhill from here.

131
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:04,240
 I mean, we're not talking about houses.

132
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:05,400
 I've never seen houses like this.

133
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:06,480
 Like to give you some reference,

134
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:08,320
 one of our neighbors was a guy named Dirk Nowitzki.

135
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,160
 Anybody ever heard of Dirk Nowitzki?

136
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:12,160
 Another neighbor, there you go.

137
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:13,880
 Big Mavs fan, rest in peace, Luca.

138
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:17,120
 All right, another neighbor was a guy named Ross Perot.

139
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:18,640
 Now you don't know him, but your parents do.

140
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,440
 Big ears, ran for president, billionaire.

141
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:23,000
 Another one of our neighbor, you don't know his name,

142
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,040
 but he founded something

143
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:27,200
 and created something called the Hefty Bag.

144
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,960
 I mean, these homes were unlike anything I've ever seen.

145
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:31,360
 And I'm sitting there talking to the son

146
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,040
 who lives in the house, getting to know him.

147
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:35,120
 And I'm trying to break down like,

148
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,100
 does he know this is not like

149
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:39,780
 what the real world is like out there?

150
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:42,800
 People don't have batting cages in their side yard

151
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,400
 and their own basketball court,

152
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:46,820
 which two of our neighbors both had.

153
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:48,800
 And I'm talking about these houses and he goes,

154
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:53,800
 man, none of those houses compare to the one two doors down.

155
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:57,040
 And he begins to describe this gigantic home.

156
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:58,720
 I mean, he lists out some of the things

157
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:02,280
 that this house that was built in 2002

158
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:06,200
 for today's equivalent of $85 million.

159
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,720
 It's the most expensive home ever built

160
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:10,320
 in our country at the time.

161
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:13,520
 It was 70,000 square feet.

162
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,680
 Now the average American home is 2,000 square feet.

163
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:21,540
 This was 35 of those combined all together.

164
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:23,480
 This was a home that the master suite,

165
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:27,060
 the master bedroom alone was 3,000 square feet.

166
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:28,440
 That's bigger than my house.

167
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:31,920
 Complete with two marble tubs

168
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,760
 made from a single block of Carrara marble

169
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:37,760
 in Verona, Italy, two of them matching.

170
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:39,800
 In case you and your wife want to take a bath

171
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,360
 at the same time, but you want to be in your own bath,

172
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:44,480
 which is not a problem I've ever had once,

173
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:47,960
 but they had it.

174
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,960
 They had a 17,000 square foot basement,

175
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:56,800
 a 16 car garage with its own indoor car wash,

176
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:59,320
 which is crazy when the cars are dirty,

177
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:01,860
 but going outside is simply not an option

178
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:03,660
 because the weather's not cooperating.

179
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:05,100
 So your cars deserve better than that.

180
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:06,160
 We got it right inside.

181
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:08,600
 A wine cellar that held 20,000 bottles of wine

182
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:11,440
 in its own dedicated tasting room, 20,000 bottles.

183
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:14,880
 This man could have drank a bottle of wine every day

184
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:16,960
 and it would have taken him 54 years

185
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:18,760
 to finish his wine cellar.

186
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:19,860
 This man won wine.

187
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:23,400
 He had, this is my favorite one.

188
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:25,300
 The house has a gift wrapping room.

189
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:30,840
 Now stay with me here because the reason this hit me

190
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:35,200
 is it's a room in the house, four walls, door, ceiling,

191
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:39,180
 dedicated exclusively to wrapping gifts.

192
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:41,400
 What level of wealth do you have to achieve

193
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:44,120
 before you look around at your family and you say,

194
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,000
 "We can't wrap presents in the living room.

195
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,500
 "What are we, animals?

196
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:49,760
 "We need a gift wrapping room."

197
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:52,600
 Whatever that level is, this man had already reached it.

198
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,960
 Four pool table rooms, not one pool table room,

199
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,160
 not one room with a pool table,

200
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:57,920
 four separate rooms, each with their own pool table.

201
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:00,160
 The grand staircase alone costs $300,000,

202
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:02,920
 its own 21 seat home theater.

203
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,040
 So you can have 20 guests over to watch a movie

204
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:07,160
 and nobody has to sit on the floor.

205
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:08,680
 And he's describing all this and I'm like,

206
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:10,280
 "Dude, that is crazy."

207
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:11,560
 It's like, "You wanna see it?"

208
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:12,760
 'Cause our property line backs up.

209
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:13,800
 We can go behind and we can go see it.

210
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:14,640
 I'm like, "Yeah, why not?

211
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:17,640
 "What does 70,000 square feet even look like?"

212
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:18,800
 So we walk over there to see it.

213
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:20,080
 And the reason I couldn't see it from the street

214
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:21,360
 was it had these giant walls,

215
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:23,560
 like it was a part of Buckingham Palace or something.

216
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:25,720
 You couldn't see over.

217
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:27,000
 And we walk behind the property line

218
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,640
 and pull up to the house and he points

219
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:33,000
 and there's nothing there

220
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,440
 but a slab with just this intense fire mark

221
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:38,200
 that had been there.

222
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:40,400
 Turned out two weeks before anyone ever moved into the house

223
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:44,840
 they were putting 50,000 gallons of stain, wood stain,

224
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:46,520
 on the top third floor of the home

225
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:49,280
 and these giant windows acted like magnifying glasses

226
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:54,240
 and the entire thing went up in flames.

227
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:58,480
 The largest single resident loss in Dallas history.

228
00:08:58,480 --> 00:08:59,320
 Now, what does that have to do

229
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:00,800
 with the one I'm talking about tonight?

230
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:02,600
 Well, what's so tragic about that scenario

231
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:04,720
 is that home is amazing as it is to hear about

232
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:07,520
 and gawk at and hear different aspects of it.

233
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,720
 What's tragic is that home never fulfilled the purpose

234
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,360
 for which it was created.

235
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:13,520
 In fact, to this day, they've never rebuilt.

236
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,400
 It got lost in a legal insurance battle

237
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:16,920
 but that home was never created

238
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:18,640
 just to be gawked at at a stage.

239
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:20,080
 On a Tuesday night it was created

240
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:21,560
 and those rooms were created to be lived in.

241
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:24,840
 That car wash was created to have cars that would wash.

242
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:26,720
 The pools, by the way, it had two,

243
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,760
 a lap pool and a volleyball pool

244
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:31,240
 'cause you can't do laps in the volleyball pool,

245
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:32,800
 but it was meant to have people swimming in it.

246
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,780
 It never fulfilled its purpose.

247
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:37,880
 Well, as tragic as that scenario is,

248
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:40,800
 there's a far greater and more prolific tragedy

249
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:41,840
 as it relates to purpose

250
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:44,120
 that permeates all throughout this room

251
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:47,380
 that is far more common

252
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:50,220
 and that's not a house not fulfilling its purpose,

253
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:51,160
 it's a person.

254
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:55,480
 I mean, reality of the home is you can get another home.

255
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:57,040
 You don't get another you.

256
00:09:57,040 --> 00:10:00,160
 You don't get another shot at your 20s or your 30s.

257
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:01,240
 You don't get another life.

258
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,280
 And sadly, many people who sit in churches

259
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:04,800
 just like this one and attend church

260
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:05,980
 and would call themselves Christians,

261
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:07,120
 they go their entire life

